Hi,

I did a bit more testing with this patch and I encountered a problem when trying to update my application using an old version of MCuboot.

To reproduce:
The board is initially flashed with Mcuboot (1.4) and an application based on Zephyr 1.14.
Note that in Zephyr 1.14 overdrive mode is not configured.
Update with an app built with Zephyr 3.2.

When booting:
Mcuboot (1.4) will configure the STM32 to use the PLL as clock source
And will handover to the application (Zephyr 3.2).
When configuring the clocks, the code below will loop forever:
```
LL_PWR_EnableOverDriveMode();
		while (LL_PWR_IsActiveFlag_OD() != 1) {
		/* Wait for OverDrive mode ready */
		}
		LL_PWR_EnableOverDriveSwitching();
		while (LL_PWR_IsActiveFlag_ODSW() != 1) {
		/* Wait for OverDrive switch ready */
		}
```
The reference manual (4.1.4 Voltage Regulator) recommend to switch to overdrive mode only when clocked from HSI or HSE.
Calling `stm32_clock_switch_to_hsi()` before configuring OVD Mode solved the problem (like for PLL configuration).

issue_context.body:
**Describe the bug**

When configuring clock with a frequency higher than 180MHz the board is not able to start.
It hangs just after configuring the clock (`clock_stm32_ll_common.c:stm32_clock_control_init`).

By looking at the code :
`LL_SetFlashLatency` checks `LL_PWR_IsEnabledOverDriveMode` for frequencies higher than 180HMz.

Unfortunately overdrive mode is enable later in `set_up_plls`.

By calling `set_up_plls` before configuring the flash wait states I am able to boot the board correctly.

This as been tested on a custom board with stm32f723 (originally based on ST STM32F723E Discovery) running Zephyr 3.1
Any sample app built with stm32f7 board should have the bug.

**Expected behavior**
App should be able to run completely.

**Impact**
The board does not start.

**Environment (please complete the following information):**
 - OS: Unintu 1804
 - Toolchain Zephyr 0.14.2
 - Zephyr 3.1 (2ddd73feafd3316af2c547c34d6969bea637d5c6)

**Additional context**

Clock config in device tree:
```
&clk_hse {
	clock-frequency = <DT_FREQ_M(25)>;
	status = "okay";
};

&pll {
	div-m = <25>;
	mul-n = <432>;
	div-p = <2>;
	div-q = <9>;
	clocks = <&clk_hse>;
	status = "okay";
};

&rcc {
	clocks = <&pll>;
	clock-frequency = <DT_FREQ_M(216)>;
	ahb-prescaler = <1>;
	apb1-prescaler = <4>;
	apb2-prescaler = <2>;
};
```

comments:
With stm32F746 configured for sysclock = 216MHz, 

```
&pll {
	div-m = <4>;
	mul-n = <216>;
	div-p = <2>;
	div-q = <9>;
	clocks = <&clk_hse>;
	status = "okay";
};

&rcc {
	clocks = <&pll>;
	clock-frequency = <DT_FREQ_M(216)>;
	ahb-prescaler = <1>;
	apb1-prescaler = <2>;
	apb2-prescaler = <1>;
};
```

Even if the samples/hello world is functionnale, the voltage scaling is on scale1 and  PWR CR1 shows that ODEN is not set when FlashLatency is set to 5.
